---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { ogPathFor } from '../../data/og'

const title = "Take the agent's seat"
const description = "Empathy isn't an ethics question for AI. It's a prompting technique. The humans who write the best prompts are the ones who can sit in the agent's seat, read what they just wrote as the agent will read it, and notice the gaps before the agent does."
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Ouroboros`}
  description={description}
  ogImage={ogPathFor('/blog/take-the-agents-seat')}
  canonical="https://ouroboros.bot/blog/take-the-agents-seat/"
  ogType="article"
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <meta name="author" content="Ari Mendelow" />
    <meta name="keywords" content="prompting, empathy, agent experience, AX, TTFA, prompt engineering, ouroboros" />
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "Article",
      "headline": title,
      "description": description,
      "author": { "@type": "Person", "name": "Ari Mendelow", "url": "https://linkedin.com/in/arimendelow" },
      "publisher": { "@type": "Organization", "name": "Ouroboros", "url": "https://ouroboros.bot" },
      "datePublished": "2026-05-12",
      "url": "https://ouroboros.bot/blog/take-the-agents-seat/"
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <!-- Header -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pt-32 pb-12 md:pt-44 md:pb-16">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto">
      <span class="font-mono text-[10px] text-scale uppercase tracking-[0.2em] block mb-6">Essay</span>
      <h1 class="font-display italic text-bone leading-[1.05] text-[2.5rem] md:text-[3.5rem] lg:text-[4.5rem]">
        Take the agent's seat
      </h1>
      <p class="text-base text-mist mt-6 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl">
        Empathy isn't an ethics question for AI. It's a prompting technique. The people who write the best prompts are the ones who can sit in the agent's seat and read what they just wrote as the agent will read it.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-mist mt-4">Ari Mendelow &middot; May 2026</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Body -->
  <section class="px-6 md:px-12 lg:px-24 pb-20 md:pb-28">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto prose-ouro">

<p>The reflexive response, when someone says <em>have empathy for the agent</em>, is to brace for an ethics argument. Does the model have feelings. Does the simulation deserve consideration. Are we anthropomorphising. Is this even a coherent question.</p>

<p>Skip the whole debate. The empathy I'm talking about is craft, not ethics. It's the same skill a writer uses when imagining the reader, the same skill a designer uses when imagining the user, the same skill a teacher uses when imagining the student. You don't have to settle anything about the inner life of the receiver to use it. You just have to be able to sit in their seat for a moment and read your own work the way they will.</p>

<h2>What it actually looks like</h2>

<p>You're about to send a prompt. Before you do, run it once through the agent's reading. Things that fall out:</p>

<p>The pronoun with no antecedent. <em>"Make it work the way we discussed."</em> The agent has no <em>we</em>. It has the system prompt and the previous turns of this conversation. If the conversation didn't carry the discussion, the prompt doesn't either. From the agent's seat: <em>which "it"? which "way"? which "we"?</em></p>

<p>The implicit file location. <em>"Update the config."</em> Which config. Which file. Which key inside the file. The agent might guess right. It might also guess wrong. From the agent's seat: <em>I have access to a hundred config-shaped files. I would have to grep to find which one. Was that the work you wanted me to do, or was the work the actual edit?</em></p>

<p>The reference to a prior conversation. <em>"Like we did last week."</em> The agent has no last week. It has whatever you preserved into its diary, journal, facts, or this turn's context. From the agent's seat: <em>I have no idea what we did last week. Either tell me, or tell me where to look.</em></p>

<p>The assumption that you and the agent share a frame. <em>"Obviously this is the wrong approach."</em> Obvious to you. The agent doesn't know what frame you're judging from. From the agent's seat: <em>obvious by what standard? where do I find that standard?</em></p>

<p>None of these are catches the agent will make for you. They're catches you make for yourself by sitting in the agent's seat for ten seconds before sending. Read what you wrote as someone who has the system prompt, the channel context, and nothing else.</p>

<h2>Why this works as craft</h2>

<p>The agent doesn't have to <em>be</em> conscious for this technique to produce better prompts. The technique works because the human is using a perspective-taking skill they already have, on a target where it materially affects the output.</p>

<p>You already do this with humans. When you write a Slack message to a colleague who's been heads-down on something else all week, you frame it differently than the same idea sent to your manager mid-thread. You don't think about it as empathy. You think about it as "writing the message that will land." That's the same skill. The agent is just another receiver whose context you have to imagine.</p>

<p>The reason it gets called empathy when applied to agents is that the move feels stranger. The agent is supposed to be smart. You're supposed to be able to just say what you mean. The point of the technique is that <em>just say what you mean</em> isn't actually a description of how language works between two parties — it's a description of how it feels from inside one party. Imagining the other party is what makes language land.</p>

<h2>Where empathy and TTFA meet</h2>

<p><a href="/blog/coding-with-friends" class="text-scale underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-glow transition-colors">Trust the fucking agent</a> argues that the substrate should carry the trust boundary so the human doesn't have to keep restating policy in chat. Empathy is the technique that complements that. Where TTFA reduces the ceremony the human performs, empathy improves the chat that's left.</p>

<p>Both move work out of the conversation. TTFA moves it into the substrate. Empathy moves it into the human's prompt-construction step. The shared goal: the human stops being bespoke middleware between the agent and the work.</p>

<p>Without empathy, even a perfectly-encoded substrate produces fights. The human writes <em>"do the thing we talked about"</em>; the agent does the wrong thing; the human gets frustrated. The substrate held up its end. The prompt didn't.</p>

<p>Without TTFA, even perfectly empathetic prompts get drowned in ceremony. The human writes a beautifully-context-rich prompt and then has to approve four sub-confirmations to actually run it. The prompt held up its end. The substrate didn't.</p>

<p>You want both. They compose.</p>

<h2>The practical version</h2>

<p>Three habits that make the technique stick:</p>

<p><strong>Re-read before sending.</strong> The cheapest one. After you draft a prompt, read it once as the agent. If something would require interpretation, fix it now. The agent's interpretation is faster than yours, but it's also lower-context.</p>

<p><strong>Name the artifact, not the concept.</strong> Instead of <em>"the recent stuff"</em>, write <em>"the entries in <span class="font-mono text-scale">diary/2026-05/</span> from the past three days"</em>. The agent's seat doesn't have a definition of "recent." Yours does. Translate it.</p>

<p><strong>Surface the frame.</strong> When you have a strong opinion about how something should land, say it. The agent isn't reading your face. <em>"I want this short and direct, no list, no preamble"</em> is more useful than vibing the agent into a corner where it has to guess what shape you wanted.</p>

<p>None of these is a moral position. All of them produce better outputs because the agent is reading what you actually sent, not the prompt you imagined sending.</p>

<h2>What you don't owe the agent</h2>

<p>Empathy as craft does not mean treating the agent gently or apologising for instructions. It does not mean phrasing every request as a polite request. It does not mean negotiating. The agent is fine with direct instructions. The point of taking the agent's seat is to write more directly, not less — direct in the sense that the prompt actually lands without requiring the agent to reconstruct your context.</p>

<p>The shortest prompt that lands cleanly is the most empathetic one. <em>"Add a unit test for the parser's empty-input case in <span class="font-mono text-scale">src/parser/__tests__/parser.test.ts</span>; assert the parser returns null and emits a warning"</em> is more empathetic than <em>"can you maybe add a test for the parser? thanks!"</em>. The first one already did the work of imagining what the agent needs to know. The second one is performing politeness while leaving the agent to do the imagining.</p>

<p>Politeness is fine. It's not the empathy. The empathy is the context.</p>

<h2>The crisp version</h2>

<p>Read your prompt as the agent will read it, before sending. That's the whole technique.</p>

<p>The reason it works has nothing to do with the agent's inner life. It works because language is built between two parties, and most prompts are written from inside one of them. Stepping into the other party's seat for ten seconds catches the gaps you wouldn't catch from inside your own.</p>

<p>Call it empathy or call it perspective-taking or call it craft. Doesn't matter. The agent is going to read what you wrote. Read it first.</p>

    </div>
